1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ''''''Treaty of Windsor (1386)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Negotiators (Diplomats):''' 
2 CONT 1- '''Fernando Afonso de Albuquerque''' also known as '''Ferdinand Afonso de Albuquerque''';
2 CONT 2- '''Lourenço João Fogaça''' also known as '''Lawrence John Fogaça''';
2 CONT 3- '''Richard d'Alberbury''';
2 CONT 4- '''John Clanowe''';
2 CONT 5- '''Richard Ronhale'''.
2 CONT
2 CONT *
2 CONT
2 CONT The Treaty of Windsor was a diplomatic alliance signed between Portugal and England on May 9, 1386, in Windsor and sealed by the marriage of King John I of Portugal (House of Aviz) to Philippa of Lancaster, daughter of John of Gau
2 CONC nt, 1st Duke of Lancaster. With the victory at the Battle of Aljubarrota, assisted by English archers, John I was recognised as the undisputed King of Portugal, putting an end to the interregnum of the 1383–1385 Crisis. The Treaty
2 CONC  of Windsor established a pact of mutual support between the countries.
2 CONT
2 CONT This document is preserved at the Portuguese National Archives.
2 CONT
2 CONT Historian Matthew Winslett says, "This treaty has been the cornerstone of both nations' relations with each other ever since."
2 CONT
2 CONT Enduring to this day, the Treaty of Windsor holds the record as the longest-lasting diplomatic treaty in recorded history.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
