2 CONT
2 CONT '''Henry II of Castile  ''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry II (January 13, 1334 Sevilla - May 29, 1379 Santo Domingo de la Calzada), better known as Henry of Trastámara (Enrique de Trastámara), 1st Conde de Trastámara, before his coronation, was the illegitimate son of Alfonso XI of
2 CONC  Castile and Eleanor of Guzmán, half brother to Peter of Castile. He took the throne after defeating and killing Peter in the Castilian Civil War (1366–1369).
2 CONT
2 CONT As head of a band of mercenaries, and with the aid of Bertrand du Guesclin, he drove Pedro from his throne in 1366. He was, however, defeated the next year at the Battle of Nájera, and Pedro was restored. Henry defeated Pedro at t
2 CONC he Battle of Montiel and then murdered him in 1369. Henry then went to war against Portugal and England in the Hundred Years' War. For most of his reign he had to fight off the attempts of John of Gaunt, a son of Edward III of Eng
2 CONC land, to claim the Castilian throne in right of his second wife, Pedro's daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 27 July 1350, Henry married Juana Manuel, the daughter of Juan Manuel, Duke of Peñafiel, head of a younger branch of the royal house of Castile. They had three children: John (1358–1390), Eleanor (1361–1425), wife of Charles II
2 CONC I of Navarre, and Joanna (1367–1374).
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry was the first ruler since King Ergica to use Anti-Semitism as a political tool in Spain. This led to an end to the convivencia, and a period of riots and pogroms, and can be seen as sowing the seeds of the persecution of the
2 CONC  Jews by the Spanish Inquisition, beginning a hundred years later.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------
2 CONT ====Family
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT On 27 July 1350, Henry married Juana Manuel, the daughter of Juan Manuel, Prince of Villena, head of a younger branch of the royal house of Castile. They had three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT # John I (1358–1390)
2 CONT # Eleanor (1361–1425), wife of Charles III of Navarre
2 CONT # Joanna (1372–1376), died young
2 CONT
2 CONT He had several children outside wedlock, some of which he mentioned in his will dated 29 May 1374:[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT # Alfonso Enriquez, Count of Gijón and Noreña (1355 – c. 1400), a son of Elvira Íñiguez,[note 1] Count of Noreña and Gijón. He married Isabel of Portugal, Lady of Viseu.
2 CONT # Leonor de Castilla, daughter of Leonor Álvarez, from Dueñas, Palencia.
2 CONT # Juana de Castilla (1367–¿?), daughter of Elvira Íñiguez. Her father mentioned her in his will, and said that if she did not marry Pedro de Aragón then she would inherit Urueña. Pedro was the Marquis of Villena (1362—Aljubarrota,
2 CONC  1385) and son of Alfonso de Aragón y Foix.[note 2]
2 CONT # Constanza Enríquez de Castilla. Her father did not mention her mother's name. When he wrote the will in 1374, she was engaged to Infante Denis, Lord of Cifuentes (1354–1397). However she eventually married his brother, Infante J
2 CONC ohn, Duke of Valencia de Campos (1349–1387). She inherited Alba de Tormes.
2 CONT # Fernando Enríquez de Castilla (1365–1438). His father describes him as the son of Beatriz Fernández.
2 CONT # María de Castilla (1375-c.1393), daughter of Beatriz Fernández. She married Diego Hurtado de Mendoza (Admiral of Castile), Lord of Mendoza, and Mayordomo mayor of King John II of Castile.
2 CONT # Fadrique de Castilla (1360–1394), son of Beatriz Ponce de León y Jérica. Named Duke of Benavente by his father, he died in prison in Almodóvar del Río in 1394. He married Leonor Sánchez de Castilla, illegitimate daughter of Sanc
2 CONC ho Alfonso, 1st Count of Alburquerque.
2 CONT # Beatriz de Castilla (?-1409), daughter of Beatriz Ponce de León y Jérica and Lady of Niebla. She married Juan Alonso de Guzmán in 1370 or 1371. He was Lord of Sanlúcar de Barrameda and the first Count of Niebla. Later she became
2 CONC  a nun in the San Clement Monastery in Seville, where she was buried.[note 3]
2 CONT # Enrique de Castilla (1378–1404), son of the Cordoban Lady Juana de Sousa, daughter of Vasco Alfonso de Sousa, the mayor of Córdoba, with María Gómez Carrillo, the Duchess of Medina Sidonia and Countess of Cabra. Enrique is burie
2 CONC d in the Cathedral of Córdoba.[note 4]
2 CONT # Pedro Enríquez de Castilla (?-1366). He is buried in Segovia Cathedral.
2 CONT # Isabel Enríquez de Castilla (?-c.1419), daughter of Juana de Cárcamo. She married Gonzalo Núñez de Guzmán, however, this marriage was dissolved by Pope Clemente VII. Later she entered the monastery of Santa Clara la Real de Tole
2 CONC do where she became its abbess and was buried.
2 CONT # Inés Enríquez de Castilla (?-c.1443), daughter of Juana de Cárcamo, also an abbess of the monastery at Santa Clara la Real de Toledo where she was buried.[note 5]
2 CONT # Juana Enríquez de Castilla, daughter de Juana, Lady of Cifuentes and wife of Infante Denis, Lord of Cifuentes (1354–1397), son of Peter I of Portugal and Inês de Castro, she is buried at Santa María de Guadalupe.[13]
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------- 
2 CONT
2 CONT Enrique II de Trastámara (Sevilla, 13 de enero de 1333 o a principios de 1334- Santo Domingo de la Calzada, 29 de mayo de 1379), primer rey de Castilla de la dinastía Trastámara, llamado el Fratricida o el de las Mercedes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rey de Castilla, Toledo, León, Galicia, Sevilla, Córdoba, Murcia, Jaén, el Algarve y Señor de Molina. (Ver http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enrique_II_de_Castilla.) 
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------- 
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry II of Castile From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry II (January 13, 1334 Sevilla - May 29, 1379 Santo Domingo de la Calzada), better known as Henry of Trastámara (Enrique de Trastámara), 1st Conde de Trastamara, before his coronation, was the illegitimate son of Alfonso XI of
2 CONC  Castile and Eleanor of Guzmán, half brother to Peter of Castile. He took the throne after defeating and killing Peter in the Castilian Civil War (1366–1369). As head of a band of mercenaries, and with the aid of Bertrand du Guesc
2 CONC lin, he drove Pedro from his throne in 1366. He was, however, defeated the next year at the Battle of Nájera (Navarette), and Pedro was restored. Henry defeated Pedro at the Battle of Montiel and then murdered him in 1369. Henry t
2 CONC hen went to war against Portugal and England in the Hundred Years' War. For most of his reign he had to fight off the attempts of John of Gaunt, a son of Edward III of England, to claim the Castilian throne in right of his second 
2 CONC wife, Pedro's daughter. On 27 July 1350, Henry married Juana Manuel, the daughter of Juan Manuel, Duke of Peñafiel, head of a younger branch of the royal house of Castile. They had three children: John (1358–1390), Eleanor (1361–1
2 CONC 425), wife of Charles III of Navarre, and Joanna (1367–1374). Henry was the first ruler since King Ergica to use Anti-Semitism as a political tool in Spain. This led to an end to the convivencia, and a period of riots and pogroms,
2 CONC  and can be seen as sowing the seeds of the persecution of the Jews by the Spanish Inquisition, beginning a hundred years later.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------- http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_II_of_Castile
2 CONT
2 CONT Also known as Enrique (or Henry) of Trastamara, he was the illegitimate son of King Alfonso XI, who was succeeded after he died in 1350 by his legitimate son, Peter the Cruel. Henry II took part in several revolts against his half
2 CONC -brother, but they were all unsuccessful. Henry II sought help from Du Guesclin and Peter IV of Aragon and finally succeeded in removing Peter the Cruel from the throne in 1366. Peter the Cruel responded by forming an alliance wit
2 CONC h England and, with the aid of Edward the Black Prince, defeated Henry II in battle at Najera in 1367. However, Edward the Black Prince had to depart to participate in the French campaign and, after he departed, Henry II attacked 
2 CONC and killed Peter the Cruel in battle at Montiel in 1369. Henry II was able to retain the throne despite opposition from King Ferdinand I of Portugal and John of Gaunt. John of Gaunt was the brother of Edward the Black Prince and, 
2 CONC in 1371, married Constance of Castile, the daughter of Peter the Cruel (perhaps marriage was the price Constance paid for the help of John of Gaunt, who undoubtedly used it make his own claim to Castile). Henry II successfuly resi
2 CONC sted all opposition and remained King until his death in 1379; he was succeeded by his son, John I of Castile. -------------------- CPB-pg. 280
2 CONT
2 CONT HGCRP-Tomo I-pg. 178
2 CONT
2 CONT HGCRP-Tomo IX-pg. 4 -------------------- Henry II of Castile
2 CONT
