1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Blanca I de Navarra, (¿?, Castilla, 1385 - Santa María la Real de Nieva, 1441), reina de Navarra (1425 - 1441).
2 CONT
2 CONT Hija de Carlos III el Noble y Leonor de Trastámara. Perteneciente a la Dinastía de Evreux. Heredera del trono de Navarra desde la muerte de su hermana mayor Juana (1413).
2 CONT
2 CONT En 1402 contrajo matrimonio con Martín "el Joven", rey de Sicilia y heredero de la Corona de Aragón. Muerto éste en 1409 pasó a gobernar la isla, regresó a Navarra y contrajo matrimonio en segundas nupcias con Juan II de Aragón, h
2 CONC ijo de Fernando de Antequera y hermano de Alfonso V "el Magnánimo", rey de Aragón.
2 CONT
2 CONT La abulia (pérdida de iniciativa) que le caracterizaba, permitió que Navarra quedara sometida en todo a su marido y a sus intereses, perdiendo el reino territorios fronterizos como consecuencia de la intervención armada de Juan II
2 CONC  en los asuntos de Castilla (1428-29).
2 CONT
2 CONT De la unión con Juan II de Aragón nació Carlos, príncipe de Viana, quien -según las capitulaciones matrimoniales de 1419- debía heredar el reino de Navarra a la muerte de su madre. Pero al morir doña Blanca, su esposo usurpó el tr
2 CONC ono navarro, alegando entre otras razones el testamento en el que la reina recomendaba a Carlos que no se hiciese coronar sin consentimiento de su padre.
2 CONT
2 CONT Carlos de Viana, declaró la guerra su padre (1451-1461): reclutó el apoyo de los montañeses de Beaumont (beamonteses) y del propio condestable castellano, Alvaro de Luna. Pero Juan II le derrotó una y otra vez, al tiempo que se ca
2 CONC saba con la castellana Juana Enríquez (1447) que le daría un hijo, el futuro Fernando el Católico.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Hijos  [editar]Carlos (1421 - 1461), Príncipe de Viana, casado con Inés de Cleves, sobrina de Felipe III, Duque de Borgoña. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Juana (1423 - 1425). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanca (1424 - 1464), casada con Enrique IV, rey de Castilla y León. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Leonor (1425 - 1479), sucesora al trono de Navarra, casada con Gaston IV de Foix, Conde de Foix y Vizconde de Bearn. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Precedido por:
2 CONT
2 CONT Carlos III
2 CONT
2 CONT  Reina de Navarra
2 CONT
2 CONT Período:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1425 - 1441 Sucedido por:
2 CONT
2 CONT Carlos IV y Juan II
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche I, Queen Nararre and Castile
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the daughter of King Charles III of Navarre and infanta Eleanor of Castile
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche married firstly Martin the Younger, King of Sicily and Crown Prince of Aragon. They were married by proxy on May 21, 1402 in Catania. Blanche travelled to meet her new husband and they were married in person on December 26
2 CONC , 1402. The bride was about eleven years old and the groom twenty-eight.
2 CONT
2 CONT Martin had survived his previous wife and former co-ruler Mary of Sicily and their only son. He was in need of legitimate heirs. However Martin and Blanche only had one son:
2 CONT
2 CONT They both survived their only son. Martin died on July 25, 1409 and was succeeded by his own father, Martin I of Aragon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche remained a widow for a decade. On November 6, 1419, Blance married her second husband John (Juan), duke of Peñafiel by proxy in Olite. John was the second son of Ferdinand I of Aragon and Eleanor of Alburquerque. Ferdinand
2 CONC  had succeeded his maternal uncle Martin I in 1412.
2 CONT
2 CONT Juan traveled to meet her. On June 10, 1420, they were married in person in Pamplona. Charles III died on September 8, 1425 and Blanche succeeded him as Queen regnant of Navarre. John became King Consort of Navarre in her right. T
2 CONC hey had four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche I of Navarre (6 July 1387[1] – April 1, 1441 in Santa María la Real de Nieva) was Queen of Navarre from 1425 to 1441.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Life 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Ancestors 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 Bibliography 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 References 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 External links 
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Life
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the daughter of King Charles III of Navarre and infanta Eleanor of Castile (died 1416).
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche married firstly Martin the Younger, King of Sicily and Crown Prince of Aragon. They were married by proxy on May 21, 1402 in Catania. Blanche travelled to meet her new husband and they were married in person on December 26
2 CONC , 1402. The bride was about eleven years old and the groom twenty-eight.
2 CONT
2 CONT Martin had survived his previous wife and former co-ruler Mary of Sicily and their only son. He was in need of legitimate heirs. However Martin and Blanche only had one son:
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince Martin of Sicily (1406-1407). 
2 CONT
2 CONT They both survived their only son. Martin died on July 25, 1409 and was succeeded by his own father, Martin I of Aragon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche remained a widow for a decade. On November 6, 1419, Blance married her second husband John (Juan), duke of Peñafiel by proxy in Olite. John was the second son of Ferdinand I of Aragon and Eleanor of Alburquerque. Ferdinand
2 CONC  had succeeded his maternal uncle Martin I in 1412.
2 CONT
2 CONT Juan traveled to meet her. On June 10, 1420, they were married in person in Pamplona. Charles III died on September 8, 1425 and Blanche succeeded him as Queen regnant of Navarre. John became King Consort of Navarre in her right. T
2 CONC hey had four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanca of Navarre (1420-1464), married Henry IV of Castile. The marriage was never consummated. After 13 years of marriage, Henry sought and obtained a divorce. Blanca was sent home, where her family imprisoned her, and she was la
2 CONC ter killed by poison. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Carlos of Viana (1421-1461) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Juana of Aragon (1423-1425) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor (1426-1479) Queen of Navarre (1479-1479) 
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Blanche, he kept the government of Navarre in his own hands, from the hands of their own son Charles of Viana, the rightful heir of the line of Navarrese kings.
2 CONT
2 CONT John became King of Aragon and King of Sicily, upon the death of his elder brother Alfonso V of Aragon in 1458.
2 CONT
