1 CONT Infanta Maria of Portugal (6 April 1342 - AFT 1363), pron. IPA: [m?'?i?], was a Portuguese infanta, first daughter of King Peter I of Portugal and his Queen consort Constance of Peñafiel.
1 CONT
1 CONT Maria was born in Évora on April 6, 1342. In 1354 she married Infante Fernando of Aragon, Marquis of Tortosa, son of Aragonese King Alfonso IV.
1 CONT
1 CONT Fernando was murdered by his half-brother Peter IV of Aragon in 1363. Maria then returned to Portugal where she died in the city of Aveiro in a year after 1363. She is buried in the Convent of Saint Claire of Coimbra.
0 @N6000000002188309714@ NOTE Edward of Norwich, 2nd Duke of York
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward of Norwich (1373 – 25 October 1415), 2nd Earl of Rutland, 2nd Earl of Cambridge, 2nd Duke of York and 1st Duke of Aumale, died at the Battle of Agincourt.
1 CONT
1 CONT The son of Edmund of Langley, 1st Duke of York, and his first wife Isabella of Castile. His paternal grandparents were Edward III of England and Philippa of Hainault. His maternal grandparents were King Pedro of Castile and Maria 
1 CONC de Padilla.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward is thought to have been born in Norwich. He was close to his cousin King Richard II, and was created Earl of Rutland by him in 1390, and then Duke of Aumale in 1397. This association put him out of favour after the usurpati
1 CONC on of King Henry IV, and he was deprived of his Dukedom. In 1400 he participated in a conspiracy against Henry IV, but betrayed the conspirators to the king. In 1402 he succeeded his father as Duke of York. He married a widow, Phi
1 CONC lippa de Mohun, but there were no children from their marriage.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward wrote “The Master of Game”, a translation of the most famous of the hunting treatises of the Middle Ages, the “Livre de Chasse” of Gaston Phoebus, Count de Foix, adding five chapters of his own.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward took part in King Henry V's war on France and died at the Battle of Agincourt, the major English casualty in that battle.
1 CONT
1 CONT On his death, the Dukedom did not immediately pass to his nephew, Richard Plantagenet, as Richard's father Richard of Conisburgh, 3rd Earl of Cambridge, had been attainted for treason, but the younger Richard was eventually restor
1 CONC ed to the Dukedom.
1 CONT
1 CONT As the Duke of Aumerle, he is a major character in William Shakespeare's Richard II, and he is also a minor character in Henry V. Although his death is depicted by Shakespeare and his adaptors as an act of heroism, it was in fact 
1 CONC more of an accident: along with many of the French knights, he was unable to remain upright when unhorsed in the fray and effectively died of suffocation under a pile of other men and horses.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
