1 CONC  Chapel Hill: UNC Dept. of Romance Languages, 1989.
1 CONT     * Flory, David. ' ' Count Lucanor: Don Juan Manuel in its context históricó'. Madrid: Sheets, 1995. * Giménez Soler, Andrés. ' ' Don Juan Manuel. Biography and study críticó'. Zaragoza: F. Martinez, 1932.
1 CONT     * Hammer, Michael Floyd. "Framing the Reader: Exemplarity and Ethics in the Manuscripts of the ' Count Lucanor'." Ph.D. University of Californian AT Los Angeles, 2004.
1 CONT     * Lida de Malkiel, Maria Rosa. "Three notes on Don Juan Manuel." ' ' Romance Philology' ' 4,2-3 (1950): 155-94. * Wacks, David A. "Don Yllán and the Egyptian Sorcerer: Vernacular commonality and literary diversity in medieval 
1 CONC Castile." ' ' Sefarad' ' 65,2 (2005): 413-33.
1 CONT     * MacPherson, Ian. ed. Juan Manuel: A Selection. London: Tamesis Texts Limited, 1980.
0 @N6000000002188376597@ NOTE Inês de Castro
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Inês Pérez de Castro (Inés in Spanish; 1325 – January 7, 1355) was a Galician noblewoman, daughter of Pedro Fernandez de Castro, lover and posthumously declared lawful wife of the Portuguese King Pedro I of Portugal, and therefore
1 CONC  Queen of Portugal.
1 CONT
1 CONT Inês came to Portugal in 1340 as a maid of princess Constance of Castile, recently married to prince Pedro, the heir to the throne. The prince fell in love with her and started to neglect his lawful wife, endangering the already f
1 CONC eeble relations with Castile. Moreover, Pedro's love for Inês brought the exiled Castilian nobility very close to power, with Inês's brothers becoming the prince's friends and trusted advisors. King Afonso IV of Portugal, Pedro's 
1 CONC father, disliked Inês's influence on his son and waited for their mutual infatuation to wear off, but it did not.
1 CONT A posthumous portrait of Inês de Castro
1 CONT
1 CONT Constance of Castile died in 1349. Afonso IV tried several times to arrange for his son to be remarried, but Pedro refused to take a wife other than Inês, who was not deemed eligible to be queen. Pedro's legitimate son, future kin
1 CONC g Fernando I of Portugal, was a frail child, whereas Pedro and Inês's illegitimate children were thriving; this created even more discomfort among the Portuguese nobles, who feared the increasing Castilian influence over Pedro. Af
1 CONC onso IV banished Inês from the court after Constance's death, but Pedro remained with her. After several attempts to keep the lovers apart, Afonso IV ordered Inês's death. Pêro Coelho, Álvaro Gonçalves, and Diogo Lopes Pacheco wen
1 CONC t to the Monastery of Santa Clara in Coimbra, where Inês was detained, and killed her. Pedro rebelled against his father, causing civil war.
1 CONT Inês's murder in the monastery of Santa Clara in Coimbra
1 CONT
1 CONT Pedro became king of Portugal in 1357. He then stated that he had secretly married Inês, who was consequently the lawful queen, although his word was, and still is, the only proof of the marriage. He had Inês's body exhumed and fo
1 CONC rced the entire court to swear allegiance to it as queen. She was later buried at the Monastery of Alcobaça where her coffin can still be seen, opposite Pedro's so that, according to the legend, at the Last Judgment Pedro and Inês
1 CONC  can look at each other as they rise from their graves. Both marble coffins are exquisitely sculpted with scenes from their lives and a promise by Pedro that they would be together até ao fim do mundo (until the end of the world).
1 CONT
1 CONT Inês de Castro’s history is immortalized in several plays and poems in Portuguese, such as The Lusíadas by Luís de Camões, and Spanish language, such as Reinar despues de morir by Luís Vélez de Guevara, as well as a play by French
1 CONC  playwright Henry de Montherlant called La Reine morte (The Dead Queen).
1 CONT
1 CONT There have been over 20 operas, including
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Ines de Castro by Niccolò Antonio Zingarelli (1798)
1 CONT     * Ines de Castro by Giuseppe Persiani to a libretto by Salvatore Cammarano (1835)
1 CONT     * Ines de Castro by Scottish composer James MacMillan was first performed at the 1996 Edinburgh International Festival[1]
1 CONT     * The opera Wut (Rage) in German by Swiss composer Andrea Lorenzo Scartazzini (born 1971) was performed for the first time at the Theater Erfurt, Germany, on September 9, 2006.
1 CONT
1 CONT History of Inês de Castro in a cigarette label.
1 CONT
1 CONT Inês de Castro and Pedro I had the following children:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * (Prince) Afonso (?), died young.
1 CONT     * (Princess) Beatrice, (c. 1347–1381), married Sancho of Alburquerque aka Sancho of Castile, Count of Albuquerque & Haro.
1 CONT     * (Prince) John (1349–1397), claimant to the throne during the 1383-1385 Crisis.
1 CONT     * (Prince) Denis (1354–1397), claimant to the throne during the 1383-1385 Crisis.
1 CONT
1 CONT Inês de Castro and Pedro I of Portugal were the great-great-grandparents of Ferdinand II of Aragon. Ferdinard's wife Isabella of Castile was a great-great-granddaughter of Pedro I of Portugal and his mistress Teresa Lourenço.
0 @N6000000002188376628@ NOTE Infanta Beatriz, Countess of Alburquerque
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Infanta Beatriz of Portugal (pron. IPA: [bi?'t?i?]; English: Beatrice or Beatrix) was the daughter of Portuguese King Peter I and a Castilian noblewoman called Inês de Castro that had arrived in Portugal as a maid of Constance of 
1 CONC Penafiel, recently married to Pedro I (Heir Prince at the time).
1 CONT
1 CONT Beatrice was born in Coimbra around the period of 1347-1351 and she became Countess of Alburquerque when she married Sancho, Count of Alburquerque. She died in 1381.
1 CONT
1 CONT Beatrice's entitlement to be considered an Infanta of Portugal is debatable. Some historians consider her a natural daughter of Peter I, so the title Infanta of Portugal could never be attributed to her. Other opinions refer that 
1 CONC after the death of Inês de Castro, ordered by Pedro's father King Afonso IV of Portugal, the Prince after inheriting the throne admitted that he had married Inês secretly, and because of that she was a lawful Queen of Portugal.
1 CONT
1 CONT Of Inês de Castro, Peter I had two sons and a daughter:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * (Infanta) Beatrice of Portugal (1347/1351-1381)
1 CONT     * (Infante) John of Portugal (1349-1387)
1 CONT     * (Infante) Denis of Portugal (1354-1397)
